<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ambrose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 339–397</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ambrose, Ambrose of Milan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ambrose was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 374 and one of the four original Doctors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a>. A Roman governor chosen bishop by popular acclaim, he opposed the Arians, defended the Church's independence from emperors, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> do penance after the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 390, and baptised Augustine. He became the patron saint of Milan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ambrose.jpg" alt="Ambrose" width="306" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmbroseOfMilan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ambrose was born in 339 into a Roman Christian family of Greek descent, at Augusta Treverorum, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, then the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a>. Scholars disagree on who his father was: some identify him with the praetorian prefect of Gaul Aurelius Ambrosius, others with an official named Uranius. A legend says that bees settled on his face in his cradle and left a drop of honey, taken as a sign of future eloquence. Trained in rhetoric and law, he served as Roman civil governor of Aemilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> before the people unexpectedly chose him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> by acclamation in 374.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As bishop Ambrose defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, tried to mediate between the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a>, and opposed the execution of Priscillianist heretics. After a massacre he excommunicated Theodosius, maintaining that an emperor is within the church, not over it. He advocated monasticism and asceticism, and his writing helped transmit Eastern Christian theology to the Latin West.</p><p>Despite heavy administrative duties he preached and presided at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> several times a week and dealt directly with the poor, widows, orphans and his clergy. He respected local customs, and advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> to follow the custom of the church where he found himself, since at Rome Ambrose fasted on a Saturday and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> did not. This gave rise to the saying When in Rome, do as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> do.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Ambrose wrote works of advocacy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a>, among them De Virginibus (377), the Exameron (386-390) and De officiis ministrorum (377-391), a practical guide for his clergy. De sacramentis contains the earliest witness to the present Roman Canon of the Mass, though its authorship is less certain than that of De mysteriis.</p><p>He was an important innovator in Christian hymnody. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers">Hilary of Poitiers</a> had written <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymns earlier, but only fragments survive, so Ambrose's are the earliest extant complete Latin hymns. Four, including Veni redemptor gentium, are universally accepted as his, each with eight four-line stanzas in iambic tetrameter. Ambrosian chant bears his name, but no melody in it can be attributed to him. With Augustine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and Gregory the Great he is one of the four Latin Doctors of the Church, formally declared in 1298.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ambrose” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gallienus took Pipa, daughter of the Marcomannic king Attalus, as a concubine, and after 395 the Christian queen Fritigil corresponded with <b>Ambrose of Milan</b>, who began a peace treaty with Stilicho.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The writers of the early church, the Church Fathers, shaped its doctrine: Irenaeus, Origen and Tertullian in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; Athanasius, the Cappadocian Fathers, John Chrysostom, <b>Ambrose</b>, Jerome and Augustine of Hippo in the 4th and 5th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>When Theodosius I dismantled the old cults, the senator Quintus Aurelius Symmachus protested in a letter, and <b>Ambrose</b>, Bishop of Milan, urged the rejection of his plea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian writers such as Tertullian, <b>Ambrose</b> and Augustine of Hippo, whose Confessions and City of God shaped Western thought, made Latin the language of theology.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish priest Bernard Callan put it into Irish around 1800, John Newton made a loose Protestant version, and <b>Ambrose</b> Bierce a satirical one in 1903.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Gregory of Nyssa wrote fifteen Homilies on the Song, comparing the bride to the soul and the groom to God, and Augustine, following <b>Ambrose of Milan</b>, read it as the wedding of Christ and the Church.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Augusta Treverorum, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Mediolanum, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.76,6.64&amp;year=376" aria-label="Augusta Treverorum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4703 431 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ambrose"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5153 701h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5153 701h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.76,6.64&amp;year=376">What was here in 376 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You are a man, and it has come upon you, conquer it. Sin is not done away but by tears and penitence.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter 51, to Theodosius I after the massacre at Thessalonica, 390 &#183; tr. H. de Romestin, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/340951.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The devil envied that which was your most excellent possession. Conquer him while you still possess that wherewith you may conquer.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter 51, to Theodosius I after the massacre at Thessalonica, 390 &#183; tr. H. de Romestin, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/340951.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
